WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.886
Approximately twelve hundred federally appointed judges serve in Canada.

00:00:03.886 --> 00:00:08.261
Eighty-eight federal judicial vacancies were recorded in October 2024.

00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:11.511
The average time to fill a vacancy: eighteen months.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:20.204
In October 2016, Judicial Advisory Committee reforms gave the government a majority on every committee — four of seven federal appointees.

00:00:20.204 --> 00:00:26.295
A 2013 University of Toronto study found that political donations correlate to judicial appointments.

00:00:26.295 --> 00:00:34.215
Carter v. Canada in 2015 legalized MAID — forty-four thousand nine hundred and fifty-eight deaths reported by 2022.

00:00:34.215 --> 00:00:38.920
Bedford v. Attorney General in 2013 struck down prostitution laws.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:48.854
R v. Jordan in 2016 imposed trial delay ceilings — eighteen months for provincial courts, thirty months for superior courts — collapsing thousands of cases.

00:00:48.854 --> 00:00:52.795
Bill C-75 reformed bail in 2019.

00:00:52.795 --> 00:00:58.784
The Violent Crime Severity Index increased thirty-two percent between 2015 and 2023.

00:00:58.784 --> 00:01:01.284
The Supreme Court legislates from the bench.

00:01:01.284 --> 00:01:04.249
Parliament fills the bench with political allies.

00:01:04.249 --> 00:01:06.568
The judiciary is not independent.

00:01:06.568 --> 00:01:07.704
It is captured.
